## 3. Pile 1 — defects, fixed

### 3.1 The thirteen `Accessing uninitialized slot` failures (the H31 question)

**The question.** Is a `DeadSlot` legitimately stored in a view-returning collection path (the
H29 family: an empty slot used as a value), or is a value read before it is written?

**The answer: neither, and the tests do not go through `materializeCallResult`.** Every one of
the thirteen failures reproduces with this stack (case `loops/reduce_view.cml`, pass
`std::fvm`; the other cases differ only in the graph names):

```
fromSlot<slot_t>              base.h:166      slot_value != DeadSlot
Array::get<slot_t>            array.h:550
printValue                    print.cpp:202
printSlot                     base.h:245
Frame::set<Array *>           frame.h:183
FastVMSchedPass::evalMarkedOperator_map_arr   fastvm.cpp:624
```

The `DeadSlot` is read by the **Debug frame log**, not by the program. The map creates its
result array, stores it in the frame slot, and only then calls the element function that
writes each element (`fastvm.cpp:623-641`). It stores the array first because the element call
is a safepoint: the array must be reachable while the callback runs. Between the store and the
last write, the array has elements that were never written, and they hold the allocator's
`0xDEADBEAF` fill. Nothing in the interpreter reads them: the source array is read (complete),
the result array is only re-read as a pointer (`currFrame.get<Array *>(self)` for the write
barrier). The readers are `Frame::set`'s INFO log (at the store) and, with `-vv`, the GET log
inside the loop (which prints the array after each write).

So it is **not** the H29 family. H29 was a *view* escaping a store and being read after its
thread-local cell was reused; the accumulator fix materializes at the store boundary. Here no
slot value is wrong and no view escapes: the collection is genuinely under construction, and
`<uninitialized>` is the honest text for the elements that do not exist yet. It is also not a
"legitimate empty element": the elements are not empty values, they are not there yet.

**The defect is in the instrumentation.** A diagnostic that is off (the default threshold is
`fatal`, not `info`) must not read anything. Two changes:

1. `Frame::get`/`Frame::set` build their text only when
   `Logger::ShouldEmit(LogLevel::Info, "Frame")` is true, and the SET line does not print the
   value at all — a set publishes a result, and the result of a higher-order operator is an
   array (or a struct holding one) that the operator fills afterwards. Print the index and the
   type. The GET line keeps the value; a `map` also re-reads its own result slot every round
   (to write through the relocated pointer), so with `-vv` that line can see the same array
   under construction — which is what the second change below is for.
2. `printValue` renders an unwritten element as `<uninitialized>` instead of aborting, the way
   the debugger's value reader already does. This costs nothing in Release, and it is what
   makes `-vv` usable on a program with a `map` (verified: `camel -vv --log-include Frame
   reduce_view.cml std::fvm` now exits 0 and prints `[(1, 30), (<uninitialized>, ...), ...]`).

The `fromSlot` assertion is kept: a `DeadSlot` that reaches a *program* read is still an
error. The printer's top-level check is kept too; only nested elements of a value under
construction are rendered.

Why the log was the only reader is also why Release was green: the whole path is inside
`EXEC_WHEN_DEBUG`. The nine tests are exactly the lock the coordinator asked for, and they now
pass in the debug tier (`list_transform.jit`, `list_transform_chain.jit`,
`nested_array_type.fvm`, `hof.run.{fvm,jit,prl}`, `reduce_element_view.{fvm,jit,prl}`). The
same stack also accounted for four more failures that H30's table did not count in the nine:
`capacity.scale_check`, `capacity.growth_fvm_gc_stress`, `capacity.growth_jit`,
`capacity.growth_tf_gc_stress` — all fixed by the same change.

**The cost was not only a wrong abort.** The eager text was quadratic: every `map` round
printed the source array and, with `-vv`, the result array. That is the 240 s of
`hof_compact_element_view` (10,000 elements x 4 operations) and the 80 s of `inside_recursion`
(500 rounds x 5,000 elements). The fix makes both sub-second.

**Release-visible guard.** The printer is used by `println` in every build; the ordinary-tier
`consistency.print_formats`, `mm.complex` and `feat.linear.collections_and_structs` tests
exercise it. The
frame log itself has no Release-visible effect by construction (it is compiled out), so its
regression is guarded by the same nine tests under `--tier debug`; all nine are already in
debug-tier plans, and none had to be added or changed.

### 3.2 The ten `castSlotFrom` failures

All ten (compound_assign x4, conversions x4, complex x2) come from one call site:

```
Type::castSlotFrom          src/core/type/base.cpp:228   castSafetyFrom(source) == Safe
Builder::rejectLiteralConversion   src/compile/gir/builder_diagnostics.cpp:755
Builder::visitLinkNode      src/compile/gir/builder_operators.cpp:1896
```

`rejectLiteralConversion` checks that a *conversion function* on a literal (`i32(5000000000)`,
`int(1e30)`, `int('12abc')`) has a value at compile time. It used `castSlotFrom` as a proxy
for the conversion. That is the wrong rule: `castSlotFrom` is the `as` conversion, whose CAST
node is built only where `castSafetyFrom` says `Safe`; the conversion functions deliberately
narrow and truncate (`numeric_convert.h` is their shared arithmetic). So `i32(2.9)`,
`int(2.5)`, `c64(2)` and `f32(1.5)` are legal programs that aborted at compile time in Debug.
There was no wrong value in Release (the converted value is discarded; the diagnostic only
asks whether it throws).

Fix: a new local helper `checkLiteralNumericConversion` applies the conversion functions' rule
with the same helpers they use (`checkedNarrow`, `checkedTruncate`): float to integer
truncates and faults when it does not fit, integer to `i32` faults when it does not fit,
integer to float rounds (an `f32` overflow is an infinity, not a fault), and a real to a
complex is always representable. `castSlotFrom` is not called and its assertion stays. The
complex target is why the old code also had a latent second abort (`c64(2)` would have reached
`castSlotFrom`'s "unsupported target" assert after a naive fix): `complex.values` and
`complex.fastvm` are the two tests that cover it.

**Release-visible guard.** `test/cases/compile/literals/convert_fits.cml` (four backends,
`expect_stdout_starts_with`) now also prints `int(2.9)`, `i32(2.5)` and `f32(1.5)` next to the
conversions already there, so a fix that refuses a legal conversion at compile time fails in
Release. The out-of-range refusals stay locked by `literal_conversion.float_to_int`,
`literal_conversion.int_to_i32`, `literal_conversion.text_to_int`,
`literal_conversion.text_to_float` and `literal_context.conversion_out_of_range`.

### 3.3 The four `Runtime Function graph cannot be null` (autodiff `grad_*`)

Stack: `Function::tupleType` (func.cpp:72) <- `visitGraphsInStaticSlot`
(draft_session.cpp:112) <- `collectStaticGraphRefs` <- `GraphDraftGroup::encode` <-
`Engine::gradient` (pullback.cpp:3223).

A draft group reserves a graph id (`reserve`, allocates from the context's id space) and binds
it only in `encode()` (`program_->bindOwn`). A function value the gradient build puts into a
static slot already names that reserved id (`addClosure`, pullback.cpp:304). While the group
collects the static references it reads the function's closure layout, and `tupleType()`
resolved it through `graph()` — which is null until the bind. The invariant "a live function
has a graph" is real; this function is not live yet, and the old code (before c708e1e2, which
made the function hold a program snapshot instead of a graph pointer) did not look the graph
up and worked. The fix adds `Function::closureType()`, the layout stored at creation, and the
draft encoder and its static-value cloner use it (`draft_session.cpp:115, 216, 222`). The
`tupleType()` assertion stays for callers that need the bound graph (GC, equals, clone,
runtime).

**Release-visible guard.** `loops.grad_through_loop`, `loops.grad_through_loop_lowered`,
`loops.grad_array_loop`, `loops.grad_array_loop_lowered` in `feat.linear.loops` are ordinary
behaviour tests; they check the values of six gradients and run in Release. A fix that loses
the closure layout fails them there.

### 3.4 The two `a loop body's collection slots do not match` failures

`ForwardRewrite::rewriteLoop` asserted `collectionTypes.size() == (r == 0 ? 1u : r)` before its
`dyType == nullptr` early return. `buildLoopBody` returns before the tape is built when the
loop's state value has no tangent, so its `collectionTypes` is empty by design; the assertion
was in the wrong place, not wrong. Moving it below the early returns (after `anyPort`, before
`makePlanTypes`) lets the intended refusal run: `Backward::run` then sees the loop (value type
`int`, no tangent) reading an active `w` and reports the located diagnostic at the loop
(pullback.cpp:2255-2275). Both tests now get `RuntimeDiag::RuntimeError` at
`refuse_loop_no_channel.cml:8:13` with "has no gradient channel", and under `std::macro` the
same text wrapped in `MacroEvaluationFailed`; nothing about the expectation changed.

**Release-visible guard.** The two tests are `kind = "negative"` behaviour tests with an exact
diagnostic name, line and text; they run in the ordinary tier as well.

## 4. Pile 2 — a decision this lane cannot make

### 4.1 Who owns the loop frame on a fault? (`loops.fault.fvm`, `loops.fault.prl`)

**Evidence.** `fault.cml` faults (division by zero) inside a round of a `while` loop. In Debug
the fault unwinder aborts:

```
FramePool::release           frame.h:519-524  last == frame
callBorrowed lambda          gotovm.cpp:870   releaseFrame(currFrame)
```

The top active frame is `main.loop1.next`; the frame the unwinder releases is the frame below
it. `callBorrowed`'s catch (gotovm.cpp:857-883) releases `currFrame`, then `rootActiveFrame`,
then pops `frameStack_` down to its base. The lowered loop's `next` frame is in none of those,
so the release is not LIFO. In Release the assertion is gone and `FramePool::release`
(`frame.h:528-529`) just moves the bump top down to the released frame, which silently frees
the frames above it; the case passes in Release on every backend, so the frames above are dead
at that point — but the ownership rule is not written down anywhere.

**Question.** Is a fault allowed to release a frame that is not on top (the frames above are
discarded with it), and if so, who releases the loop's `next` frame: the loop lowering (which
created it and knows the loop is being unwound) or the generic call unwinder (which would have
to walk the pool down to the target)? The strict-LIFO assertion in `FramePool` is the only
statement of the rule today.

**Options.**
1. Keep strict LIFO and let the loop lowering own its frame: catch the fault where `next` is
   called, release that frame, rethrow. The unwinder sees the expected top; the assertion
   stays meaningful; no frame above a live frame is ever discarded.
2. Make the unwinder's `releaseFrame` total: release every active frame above the target
   (walk `FramePool`), and weaken the assertion to "the target is active". Minimal code, but
   it makes "release out of order" a supported operation and loses the double-release check
   for that path.
3. Keep the assertion and fix the order (release `next` first). The unwinder does not know
   about the frame, so this needs a registry of "frames a lowering owns", i.e. option 1 in
   disguise.

**Recommendation: option 1.** The frame was created by the loop machinery, the fault is
unwound through it, and the pool's invariant is worth keeping. Option 2 is acceptable as a
stop-gap if the loop owner cannot be reached cleanly, but it should then be documented in
`frame.h` as "a release discards everything above the target". Code and expectation were left
alone in this pass.

### 4.2 The native-stack limit (handed off)

Not this lane. `loops.inside_recursion` fails four times in the debug tier: `nvm` reports
`MaxRecursionDepthExceeded` at 363 open calls ("the native stack of the thread, 32768 KiB, is
full"). Before H31 the three other backends timed out (80 s) before they got that far; with
the log fix `fvm`, `jit`, `prl` each pass on their own (verified by hand: exit 0, `12498000`)
but are compared against `nvm` and so fail with it. The language's default non-tail limit is
1000 (design principles 3.9); the Debug build cannot reach it because its interpreter frames
are about 90 KiB. The
coordinator has handed the larger `/STACK` for Debug tools to another agent; this lane did not
touch the stack settings, the case, or the plan's parameters. The three `inside_recursion`
timeouts are gone (about 0.2 s each now); they are not timeouts any more.

